"ARS COMBINATORIA 163 (2025) 29\u001549 (https://combinatorialpress.com/article/ars/Volume%20163/eternal-distance-2-domination-in-trees.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; an in\u001cnite family of trees whose eternal distance-k domination number meets our upper ... all,k(T) where T is a tree is poly- ... We conjecture ' that a similar result holds for k > 2.\neternal distance-2 domination in trees\n47\nwe know n = a + b and γ∞\nall,k(T1) =\n2a\n1+\nk\nP\n1+\nk\nP\ni=1\n∆(∆−1)i−1 and γ∞\nall,k(T2) =\n2b\ni=1\n∆(∆−1)i−1.\nIt is not hard to see that if as V = V (T1) ∪V (T2) and T1, T2 are subgraphs of T,\nγ∞\nall,k(T) ≤γ∞\nall,k(T1) + γ∞\nall,k(T2)\n=\n2a\n1 +\nkP\n1 +\nkP\ni=1\n∆(∆−1)i−1\n+\n2b\ni=1\n∆(∆−1)i−1\n=\n2n\n1 +\nkP\ni=1\n∆(∆−1)i−1\n,\nthis completes the proof.\n8.\nConclusion\nWe have resolved a number of questions regarding the eternal distance-2 domination\nnumber of trees, in particular several questions raised in [6]. We do this by giving a\npolynomial time algorithm (Theorem 3.5) for calculating the eternal distance-2 domina-\ntion of a tree, then using the reductions involved in this algorithm we characterize which\ntrees have domination number equal to their eternal distance-2 domination number (The-\norem 4.2). Beyond this we characterize which trees are eternal distance-2 domination\ncritical (Theorem 5.5) and which trees have eternal distance-2 domination number equal\nto their distance-2 domination number (Theorem 6.1). These results are similar to work\nby Klostermeyer and MacGillivray [12] who proved several characterizations for trees in\nthe context of eternal domination.\nAdditionally, we generalize upper bounds on the eternal domination number given by\nChambers, Kinnersley, and Prince [4] to the eternal distance-k domination number. We\nalso give a lower bound for the eternal distance-k domination number in terms of order and\nmaximum degree. To demonstrate that the upper bound we prove is tight we construct\nan in\u001cnite family of trees whose eternal distance-k domination number meets our upper\nbound. We conclude the paper by listing several open problems and conjectures.\nThe \u001crst of these conjectures arise from our observation that eternal distance-k domi-\nnation seems to have some fundamental di\u001berences in the k ≤2 and k > 2 cases. This is\nhighlighted by the e\u001bectiveness of Lemma 3.3 for k ≤2 and subsequent failure for k > 2.\nConjecture 8.1. For all integers k > 2, determining γ∞\nall,k(T) where T is a tree is poly-\nnomial time bounded by a polynomial p(n) independent of k.\nNext we observe that Theorem 6.1 combined with work in [17] implies that if γ2(T) =\nγ∞\nall,2(T), then γ∞\nall,2(T) = γ(T). We conjecture ' that a similar result holds for k > 2.\nRecall that γk ≤γ∞\nall,k ≤γ⌊k\n2⌋for all graphs.\nConjecture 8.2. For all k > 2 and trees T, if γk(T) = γ∞\nall,k(T), then γ∞\nall,k(T) = γ⌊k\n2⌋(T).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEternal distance-k domination on graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03835)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; For trees we use decomposition arguments to bound the eternal distance-$k$ domination numbers, and solve the problem entirely in the case of perfect $m$-ary trees.\nTitle: Eternal distance-k domination on graphs\nAuthors: Danielle Cox, Erin Meger, M. E. Messinger\nDate: Thu Apr  8 15:18:45 2021\n\nEternal domination is a dynamic process by which a graph is protected from an infinite sequence of vertex intrusions. In eternal distance-$k$ domination, guards initially occupy the vertices of a distance-$k$ dominating set. After a vertex is attacked, guards ``defend'' by each moving up to distance $k$ to form a distance-$k$ dominating set, such that some guard occupies the attacked vertex. The eternal distance-$k$ domination number of a graph is the minimum number of guards needed to defend against any sequence of attacks. The process is well-studied for the situation where $k=1$. We introduce eternal distance-$k$ domination for $k > 1$. Determining whether a given set is an eternal distance-$k$ domination set is in EXP, and in this paper we provide a number of results for paths and cycles, and relate this parameter to graph powers and domination in general. For trees we use decomposition arguments to bound the eternal distance-$k$ domination numbers, and solve the problem entirely in the case of perfect $m$-ary trees.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEternal Distance-2 Domination in Trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.00054)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; We show that finding a minimum eternal distance-2 dominating set of a tree is linear time in the order of the graph by providing a fast algorithm. ... We conclude by providing general upper and lower bounds for the eternal distance-k domination number of a graph, as well as constructing an infinite family of trees which meet said upper bound and another which meets the given lower bound.\nTitle: Eternal Distance-2 Domination in Trees\nAuthors: Alexander Clow, Christopher M van Bommel\nDate: Mon Jul 31 18:20:33 2023\n\nWe consider the eternal distance-2 domination problem, recently proposed by Cox, Meger, and Messinger, on trees. We show that finding a minimum eternal distance-2 dominating set of a tree is linear time in the order of the graph by providing a fast algorithm. Additionally, we characterise when trees have an eternal distance-2 domination number equal to their domination number or their distance-2 domination number, along with characterizing which trees are eternal distance-2 domination critical. We conclude by providing general upper and lower bounds for the eternal distance-k domination number of a graph, as well as constructing an infinite family of trees which meet said upper bound and another which meets the given lower bound.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nALEX CLOW, Simon Fraser University (https://www2.cms.math.ca/Events/winter24/abs/pdf/ttp-ac.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Eternal Distance-k Domination in Trees\nALEX CLOW, Simon Fraser University\nEternal Distance-k Domination in Trees\nThis talk considers the eternal distance-k domination problem, a variant of the eternal domination problem where guards can\nmove any distance t ∈{0, 1, . . . , k} on their turn. We prove upper and lower bounds for the eternal distance-k domination\nnumber of a graph in terms of order, maximum degree, and k, before showing that both bounds are tight for trees. The rest\nof the talk will present open conjectures regarding the eternal distance-k domination number of trees, along with evidence to\nsupport these conjectures.\nThis is joint work with Christopher van Bommel (University of Guelph).\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNeural Bonanza III: The Final Bonanza Pt. 1 (https://artsci.tamu.edu/mathematics/_files/_docs/reu/results/2019/gambacini.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Goldrup and Phillipson Conjecture ... Then the Conjecture says C is not closed convex... but this is false!\nGoldrup and Phillipson Conjecture\nOpen convex\nNot max-∩-complete\n135 ∩236 = 3 /∈C\n≥2 non-mandatory words\nLk{3}(∆) = {15, 12, 26, ∅}\nLk{4}(∆) = {12, ∅}\n≥3 facets M1, M2, M3\nC = {1, 13, 14, 135, 123, 12,\n124, 236, 23, 24, 2, ∅}\nM1 = 123, M2 = 236, M3 = 135\nσ ∈C such that:\nσ ⊂M1. Let σ = 13. 13 ⊂123\nσ ∩M2 /∈C. 13 ∩236 = 3 /∈C\nThen the Conjecture says C is not closed convex... but this is false!--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/338456/pointed-e-positivity-conjecture-for-unit-interval-graphs)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; # Pointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs ... A pointed symmetric function is pointed e-positive when it is positive in the pointed elementary-symmetric basis.\n\n# Pointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs\n\n8 years old · traced to 2018\n\nLet $m=m_1\\cdots m_n$m=m 1​⋯m n​ satisfy $1\\leq m_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq m_n\\leq n$1≤m 1​≤⋯≤m n​≤n and $m_i\\geq i$m i​≥i for each $i$i, and let $G(m)$G(m) be the graph on $[n]$[n] with an edge $(i,j)$(i,j) exactly when $i<j\\leq m_i$i<j≤m i​. Let $X_{G(m),1}$X G(m),1​ be the pointed chromatic symmetric function with distinguished vertex $1$1. A pointed symmetric function is pointed e-positive when it is positive in the pointed elementary-symmetric basis. Unit-interval pointed e-positivity conjecture. $X_{G(m),1}$X G(m),1​ is pointed e-positive. This would provide a pointed analogue of the elementary-positivity phenomenon sought for incomparability graphs of $(\\mathbf{3}+\\mathbf{1})$(3+1)-free posets; the supplied text does not state a proof or resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Brendan Pawlowski, “Chromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n”, arXiv:1802.05470 (2024).\n\nchromatic-symmetric-functionssymmetric-functionsunit-interval-graphs\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNon-monotonicity of closed convexity in neural codes (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.00963)\nciteturn2academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Finally, we disprove a conjecture of Goldrup and Phillipson, and also present an example of a code that is neither open convex nor closed convex.\nTitle: Non-monotonicity of closed convexity in neural codes\nAuthors: Brianna Gambacini, R. Amzi Jeffs, Sam Macdonald, Anne Shiu\nDate: Mon Dec  2 17:51:33 2019\n\nNeural codes are lists of subsets of neurons that fire together. Of particular interest are neurons called place cells, which fire when an animal is in specific, usually convex regions in space. A fundamental question, therefore, is to determine which neural codes arise from the regions of some collection of open convex sets or closed convex sets in Euclidean space. This work focuses on how these two classes of codes -- open convex and closed convex codes -- are related. As a starting point, open convex codes have a desirable monotonicity property, namely, adding non-maximal codewords preserves open convexity; but here we show that this property fails to hold for closed convex codes. Additionally, while adding non-maximal codewords can only increase the open embedding dimension by 1, here we demonstrate that adding a single such codeword can increase the closed embedding dimension by an arbitrarily large amount. Finally, we disprove a conjecture of Goldrup and Phillipson, and also present an example of a code that is neither open convex nor closed convex.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Eternal Distance-2 Domination in Trees (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372827352_Eternal_Distance-2_Domination_in_Trees)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; 7 Extremal Families of Trees for Eternal Distance-k Domination ... Suppose then that G=T= (V, E) is a tree.\nv 3 are dominated, and |D′|<|D|, contradicting that γ∞\n\nall,2(T) = γ 2(T). Hence all neighbours of v 4 are\n\nleaves in T, and therefore v 4 is a stem. Let T′=T−L[v 3]. We can verify that γ∞\n\nall,2(T′) + 1 = γ∞\n\nall,2(T)\n\nand γ 2(T′) + 1 = γ 2(T). Thus, γ∞\n\nall,2(T′) = γ 2(T′), so by the induction hypothesis, T′∈T. Since T is\n\nobtained from T′by operation T 3, we conclude T∈T.\n\n7 Extremal Families of Trees for Eternal Distance-k Domination\n\nMotivat